# Git Workflow

MUST: GitHub issue → branch → PR → human merges. Never commit to the default branch.

Before commit, push, or PR: ask **"Anything to add before I commit?"** and wait.
Do not commit, push, or create/close/comment on issues/PRs unless explicitly asked.

## Branch, commit, label

- Branch: `{type}/{issue}-{short-slug}`
- Commit: `{type}: {present-tense summary} (#{issue})`
- Issue: exactly **one** type label matching the commit prefix

Types / labels (required — create with `gh label` if missing; do not substitute `bug` / `enhancement`):

`feat` | `fix` | `docs` | `chore` | `refactor` | `sec` | `breaking`

```
✅ feat/11-vm-snapshot-list
   feat: add snapshot list to manage page (#11)
   label: feat

❌ branch: my-fixes / fix-stuff
❌ chore: misc updates
❌ commit without (#N) or without an issue
❌ primary label: bug / enhancement (use fix / feat)
```

Triage labels (`help wanted`, `dependencies`, `question`, …) may accompany the type label.

After merge: checkout default branch, pull, delete local feature branch.

## PRs

```
✅ One concern; PR body explains why
✅ Run this repo's local checks before push (see stack/style rules)
✅ Update project docs if behavior changed

❌ Kitchen-sink PR
❌ "CI will catch it" without running local checks
```

If the repo has a `CONTRIBUTING.md`, follow it for human-oriented detail — this rule is the agent source of truth for git shape.
